main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member-template deduction from const funptr arg: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*no body for callee.*$
--
N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/3: for a forwarding reference Fn&& and an lvalue
argument of type `const FP` (a const function pointer), Fn is deduced as
`const FP&`, so the deduced parameter `const FP&` binds a const function-pointer
lvalue.  CBMC drops the const while building the deduced reference parameter
(it becomes `FP&`), so the const argument no longer binds, the sole (template)
candidate is discarded during overload resolution, and -- because this happens
during the elaboration of an instantiated member body -- the call resolution
silently fails (via the "all candidates are templates" SFINAE-tolerance path in
cpp_typecheck_resolvet::resolve) and the enclosing member Mgr<FP>::clone is left
with no body.

This is the shape of libstdc++ std::function's
_Function_base::_Base_manager<F>::_M_manager __clone_functor case, which calls
_M_init_functor(__dest, *const_cast<const _Functor*>(...)) with _Functor a
function pointer; the silently-discarded body is why a single
`std::function<int(int)> f = &fn;` reports _M_manager as no-body.

KNOWNBUG until the deduced const-qualified function-pointer reference parameter
is built with its const preserved; flip to CORE once it verifies.
